  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-2)\\,\u2295\\,{J}_{1}(1)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-2,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(1) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only \n$z$\nis an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+2I)z=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition.",
